<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khambhat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cambay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khambhat, historically Cambay, is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat">Gulf of Khambhat</a>. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it was one of the great ports of western India, exporting textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, and it was visited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> and many later European travellers. Silting of the gulf later ruined its harbour, and from about 1730 it was the seat of the Nawabs of Cambay.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khambhat.jpg" alt="Khambhat" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dhruv855 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_of_Khambaht(Tower_Area).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Khambhāt lies on an alluvial plain at the northern end of the Gulf of Khambhāt, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anand-district">Anand district</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. The gulf is noted for the extreme rise and fall of its tides, which can vary by as much as thirty feet near the town. The soil is silt deposited by the Mahi River. Some have thought the city to be the Camanes of Ptolemy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-tod">James Tod</a> derived its name from the Sanskrit Khambavati, the City of the Pillar. The town is known for its halvasan sweet, sutarfeni, agate stone and kites.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Cambay was a flourishing city and the seat of an extensive trade, noted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, chintz and gold stuffs. The Arab traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-masudi">al-Mas'udi</a> visited in 915 and called it a very successful port. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>, who called it Cambaet, noted in 1293 that it was a busy port with a king of its own. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">Indigo</a> and fine buckram were products of the region, and much <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and leather was exported. In the early 1340s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> remarked on its architecture and cosmopolitan population, observing that most inhabitants were foreign merchants who competed in building houses and mosques. Niccolò de' Conti, visiting about 1440, gave the circumference of its walls as twelve miles.</p><p>The Portuguese Duarte Barbosa, in the early sixteenth century, found a busy and rich city of Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, with ships sailing to the Sultanate of Mogadishu with cloths and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and returning with gold, wax and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>. The difficulty of reaching the town as the gulf silted up gradually ended its commerce, which by 1900 was chiefly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>. Cambay State, a princely state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> with an area of 350 square miles, was founded in 1730 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> broke up. General Goddard took Cambay in 1780, it was restored to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> in 1783, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> ceded it to the British by the treaty of 1803.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Khambhāt was long a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and holds old Jain temples and collections of Jain palm-leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. It is perhaps the only place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> where the Harappan craft of agate bead making survives as a living tradition, although the town has no stone deposits of its own and obtains its stones from the Rajpipla hills, about 200 km away. Folklore credits the craft to Baba Ghor, a saint from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, while the archaeological record traces it to the nearby Harappan outpost of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothal">Lothal</a>. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> gave the town 201,964 inhabitants, of whom 72.88 per cent were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, 23.87 per cent Muslims and 2.50 per cent Jains.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khambhāt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the town had grown considerably: the Portuguese writer Duarte Barbosa, writing a few years after the first European contacts, described Mombasa as a large and handsome town of stone and mortar houses, with a busy harbour full of ships and an active trade with Sofala, <b>Cambay</b> and Arabia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>As imperial authority collapsed he made himself master of the ancient port of <b>Cambay</b> (Khambhat) around 1730, founding a line of Nawabs who survived Maratha pressure by paying them a share of the revenue.</blockquote><a href="/state/cambay-state">Cambay State</a></li><li><blockquote>Surat became the headquarters of the directorate of Suratte, which also oversaw posts at Ahmedabad, Agra and <b>Cambay</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-suratte">Dutch Suratte</a></li><li><blockquote>Gujarat's ports, including <b>Cambay</b>, Surat and Diu, were hubs of the Indian Ocean trade in textiles, indigo and spices.</blockquote><a href="/state/gujarat-sultanate">Gujarat Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Gujarat's ports, notably <b>Cambay</b>, traded across the Arabian Sea.</blockquote><a href="/state/vaghela-dynasty">Vaghela dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
